 I used complex numbers to represent the trigonometric functions. Starting with
\begin{align*}\cos 5 \theta + i \sin 5 \theta &= (\cos \theta + i \sin \theta)^5\\
&= \cos^5 \theta + 5i \cos^4 \theta \sin \theta - 10 \cos^3 \theta \sin^2 \theta\\
&\qquad - 10i \cos^2 \theta \sin^3 \theta + 5 \cos \theta \sin^4 \theta + i \sin^5 \theta.\end{align*}
The real part this equals $\cos^5 \theta - 10 \cos^3 \theta \sin^2 \theta + 5 \cos \theta \sin^4 \theta$ and the imaginary part equals $5 \cos^4 \theta \sin \theta - 10 \cos^2 \theta \sin^3 \theta + \sin^5 \theta.$  Since $\sin \theta = \pm \sqrt{1 - \cos^2 \theta},$ we can plug these values into the previous equation to get
\begin{align*}\cos^5 \theta + 5(1 - \cos^2 \theta)\cos^3 \theta \pm 10(1 - \cos^2 \theta) \cos \theta (1 - \cos^2 \theta)\\
- 5 \cos^5 \theta \pm \sqrt{1 - \cos^2 \theta} \sqrt{(1 - \cos^2 \theta)^3}\end{align*}
Solving gives
\begin{align*}\cos^5 \theta - 10 \cos^3 \theta \pm 5 \cos \theta \pm \sqrt{1 - \cos^2 \theta} \sqrt{(1 - \cos^2 \theta)^3}\end{align*}
Since $\cos^5 \theta = \cos^5 \theta - 10 \cos^3 \theta \pm 8\cos \theta,$ comparing real parts with Euler's formula gives
\begin{align*}\cos^5 \theta &= \cos \theta - 8\cos 5 \theta + 10 \cos 3 \theta\end{align*}
Comparing coefficient gives $a_1=1,\, a_2=-8,\, a_3=10,\, a_4=0,\, a_5=0.$  Then $a_1^2 + a_2^2 + a_3^2 + a_4^2 + a_5^2 = \boxed{165}.$</answer>

